# LIVE REGRESSION FIXTURE (graduated out of `known_gaps/`) — the
# `&`-in-an-OWNING-POSITION class, costume 6 of 7: a TUPLE-LITERAL ELEMENT
# (`(&c.fd, 5)`).
#
# REJECTED at `gg check` as intended. Tuple field init is an ownership boundary
# (devbook/11 lists tuple/struct field init together as "unconditional
# leave-behinds" cloned via `ensure_owned_at_boundary`).
#
# ⚠ THE CODE IT FIRES IS NOT THE CODE THIS CLASS IS OWED. The reject arrives as
# `error[E_AmpInOperandPosition]` — the OPERAND-position rule, not a dedicated
# ownership-BOUNDARY diagnostic. Correct and actionable here (a tuple-literal
# element has no callee), but three siblings of this class — ctor / push / enum
# payload — are STILL ACCEPTED at HEAD because they DO have a callee. This
# fixture's green does not close the class; the parent TODO row stays open.
#
# ⚠ LIKE THE ARRAY-LITERAL COSTUME, THIS WAS A GARBAGE-VALUE MISCOMPILE, not a
# dropped sigil. Before the reject: `gg check` clean, both backends accepting,
# and a RAW STACK ADDRESS in the `int` slot:
#     C    -> 281474930661888
#     LLVM -> 281474478852616
# (digits are ASLR-dependent; the stable fact is that it is a pointer, not 10).
# `ggdef` REJECTS it too ("`&`-alias into an owning position is not valid").
#
# The `fd` seed is COMPUTED, not a literal.
#
# See `sound_amp_owning_position_ctor.gg` for the full sibling list and the
# ruling this class rests on.
struct Counter:
    int fd

int seed():
    return 10

void main():
    Counter c = Counter(seed())
    (int, int) t = (&c.fd, 5)
    print(t.0)
